The corrected code:
class Animal {
    private String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void speak() {
        System.out.println(name + " makes a sound.");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    @Override
    public void speak() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks.");
    }
}

1. Why does name = name; not set the object's field?
the 'name' refers to the constructor's parameter not the object's field

2. How can this fix the problem?
By adding the this keyword so you can refer to the object's field

3. What should the Dog constructor do to initialize the parent?
 have the super(name) to call the parent class constructor

4. Which annotation should be used for the overridden method?
the @Override annotation